r normal intervals start <= end, start < end for non-zero length, but zero-length [1,1] would duplicate. But even for normal, if first interval [1,3], no bug. But if intervals sorted by start, first current is same as merged[0], condition current[0] < last[1] true if start < end, so no duplicate. But if interval [1,1] duplicate. Is that considered bug? The loop should start from intervals[1:] to avoid processing first interval. This is a bug, especially for zero-length intervals. But maybe they expect at least two bugs: sort key wrong and loop should skip first. Another bug: overlap condition should be <= for touching intervals? User specifically includes touching intervals like [1,3] and [3,5]. Usually merge overlapping intervals may or may not merge touching. They say merge overlapping intervals, but test includes touching intervals like [1,3", "top2": [[[1596, -0.00146376877091825], [760, -7.693026542663574]], [[1144, -1.0728841743912199e-06], [599, -14.604830741882324]], [[4087, -0.005713061895221472], [5707, -5.214104175567627]], [[1156, -0.0012382779968902469], [303, -6.806948184967041]], [[579, -0.5767714977264404], [13, -1.5669257640838623]], [[1622, -0.0036150512751191854], [9640, -6.8211541175842285]], [[13, -0.007905055768787861], [25, -5.193205833435059]], [[14235, -0.06706245243549347], [26472, -3.5492231845855713]], [[1423, -1.1225980520248413], [7936, -1.3287138938903809]], [[1396, -0.5428035259246826], [21851, -0.9483936429023743]], [[9584, -6.985908112255856e-05], [506, -10.193257331848145]], [[303, -0.25998494029045105], [11, -1.58892822265625]], [[12654, -0.3798139691352844], [2574, -1.7439383268356323]], [[709, -0.09389789402484894], [10562, -2.4257822036743164]], [[11, -0.4688490033149719], [10562, -1.5319136381149292]], [[10033, -0.0013720861170440912], [799, -6.879347801208496]], [[1754, -0.0013411690015345812], [799, -6.655764102935791]], [[303, -0.5630763173103333], [799, -0.8513986468315125]], [[799, -3.933914285880746e-06], [11316, -12.586